[Course "The Scotch Game"]
[ChesslyCourse "7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd"]
[CourseColor "white"]
[CourseDescription "The Scotch is one of the oldest openings in chess, dating back to the year 1750. It also was Levy's first ever chess opening! It's easy to learn and the ideas are quite fun."]
[Chapter "The Main Line (4...Nxd4)"]
[Study "Main Line Under 2000 (4...Nxd4)"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Scotch Game/01-01 The Main Line (4...Nxd4) - Main Line Under 2000 (4...Nxd4).mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{Welcome to the first chapter of the Scotch! The Scotch is a great opening choice for e4 players who want to avoid the Ruy Lopez or the Italian Game. Our opponent opts for ...e5 and ...Nc6, but we immediately spice things up in the center with d4 and often get an active, tactical position! Let's get into it, starting with 1.e4!}
1. e4 e5 {Black goes for ...e5. We'll bring the knight to f3 as is usually done in e4-e5 openings.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Black develops and protects e5. Now 3.d4 is the signature move of the Scotch! We go for central activity. 3.d4 immediately creates two threats: capturing the pawn on e5 or pushing d5 with a tempo, gaining central space. [%cal Gd2d4] [%csl Re5]} 3. d4 exd4 {Black trades on d4. This happens in nearly 90% of the games, as other moves are inferior. We'll recapture with the knight. [%csl Rd4]} 4. Nxd4 Nxd4 {Black trades again, not allowing us to double their c-pawns. This trade is the most popular move under 2000. Played nearly 45% of the time, the problem with it is that our queen arrives rapidly in the center of the board. [%csl Rd4]} 5. Qxd4 d6 {And Black goes for the most popular move, 5...d6 played in almost 40% of the games. Black opens up the light-squared bishop and most importantly, fights for the e5-square. We'll develop the knight, fighting for central squares. [%csl Re5]} (5... Nf6 {Black goes for the third most popular move 5...Nf6, which is a mistake! It allows us to immediately attack the knight with e5. [%csl Rf6]} 6. e5 Qe7 {Black defends the knight by pinning the e-pawn. We'll interfere the pin with the bishop, after which the knight on f6 is under renewed attack. [%csl Ge7,Re5,Re1]} (6... c5 {Black attacks our queen instead of retreating the knight, which occurs in around 20% of the time. We bring the queen to e3 so that ...Qe7 doesn't pin the e-pawn and simply loses the knight. [%csl Rd4]} 7. Qe3 Nd5 {Black attacks our queen. We'll bring it to e4, attacking the knight in return. [%csl Re3]} 8. Qe4 Nb4 {Black brings the knight to b4, intending to retreat to c6 in the future. We just develop the knight, aiming at weak light squares in the black camp.} (8... Nb6 {Black retreats to b6. We'll develop. Our knight aims at the weak b5 and d5 squares. [%csl Rb5,Rd5]} 9. Nc3 Be7 {Black prepares to castle. Now Bf4 reinforces the pawn on e5, aims at the weakened d6-square and prepares to castle queenside! [%csl Rd6]} 10. Bf4 O-O {Black hides their king. But we can develop a piece with a checkmate threat. [%csl Rh7]} 11. Bd3 g6 {Black prevents the mate by blocking the battery.} 12. O-O-O {After castling queenside our position is very comfortable. We have more space, better developed pieces and better control over the center. We can start to improve our pieces with Rhe1, Nd5, and Bh6. [%cal Gf4h6,Gh1e1,Gc3d5] [%csl Gc1]}) 9. Nc3 d5 {Black expands and attacks our queen. But we can capture en passant, with discovered check! [%csl Re4]} 10. exd6+ Be6 {Black blocks the check.} 11. Bb5+ {Check! And Black's position collapses shortly. They'll have to retreat the knight to c6, where it is attacked twice. [%cal Gb5c6,Ge4c6,Rb4c6] [%csl Re8]}) 7. Be3 Ng8 {Black retreats. We'll develop our queen's knight. d5 looks like a great square for it!} 8. Nc3 d6 {Black challenges our strong e5-pawn. But we'll centralize the knight and attack the queen. [%csl Rd5]} 9. Nd5 Qd7 {Black moves their queen to d7. Black's king is uncastled, so opening up the center is a great idea, and we'll take on d6. [%csl Re8]} 10. exd6 Qxd6 {Black recaptured with the queen to avoid Bb5, which would have won the queen owing to the fork on c7...} 11. Bf4 {...but this allows Bf4! We are attacking the queen, and the pawn on c7 behind it, which will fall next. White is completely winning. [%cal Gd5c7] [%csl Gf4,Rd6,Re8]}) (5... Qf6 {This is the engine's top choice and the second most popular move, played in almost 20% of the games. We don't want to trade the queens, as it would help Black with their development.} 6. Qe3 b6 {With ...b6 Black aims to quickly develop the bishop pair. We'll develop our knight, aiming at the d5-square. [%cal Gf8c5]} (6... Bb4+ {Black develops with check. We'll block with the pawn, gaining a tempo on the bishop. [%csl Re1]} 7. c3 Ba5 {Black retreats to a5. We'll develop the bishop to d3 from where it supports the e4-pawn.} 8. Bd3 Bb6 {Black attacks our queen. We'll retreat to e2, the safest square for our queen. Here the queen also defends f2. [%csl Re3]} 9. Qe2 d6 {Black fights for the e5-square. Since our queenside is yet to be developed, we'll get our king into safety by castling kingside. [%csl Re5]} 10. O-O Ne7 {Black wants to castle as well. We want to develop the queenside minor pieces quickly! Having played c3 on move seven, we no longer have c3 for our knight. [%cal Ge8g8]} 11. Nd2 O-O {Black castles.} 12. Nc4 {Nc4 aims to win the bishop pair. Once we capture the bishop on b6, we can push f4 to gain more space on the kingside and use the bishop pair to our advantage! [%cal Gc4b6,Gf2f4,Ga2a4] [%csl Gc1,Gd3,Rb6]}) 7. Nc3 Bc5 {Black attacks our queen, the bishop being supported by the b6-pawn. We'll move the queen to the side. On g3 it is placed well, since it doesn't interfere with our minor piece development. [%csl Re3]} 8. Qg3 Bb7 {Black fianchettoes. We'll develop our bishop with an attack on the c7-pawn. [%csl Rc7]} 9. Bf4 d6 {Black blocks the attack.} 10. O-O-O {Now we get our king into safety on the queenside. We'll aim to continue developing with Bb5+ and also push e5 to open up the center. [%cal Gf1b5,Ge4e5] [%csl Gc1,Re8]}) 6. Nc3 Nf6 {Black develops their knight. By developing the bishop to f4 we add control over the e5-square and prepare to castle queenside.} 7. Bf4 Be7 {Black prepares to castle short. We'll get the king into safety and centralize the rook by castling queenside.} 8. O-O-O O-O {Black also gets their king out of the center and into safety.} 9. f3 {And with the move f3, we support the pawn on e4 and prepare a kingside attack with g4 and h4! Kb1 is also a useful move in many positions where we've castled queenside. [%cal Gc1b1,Gg2g4,Gh2h4] [%csl Ge4,Gf3,Rg8]} *

[Chapter "The Classical Variation (4...Bc5)"]
[Study "Classical Scotch (4...Bc5)"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Scotch Game/02-01 The Classical Variation (4...Bc5) - Classical Scotch (4...Bc5).mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{In this study, we'll take a look at Black's fourth move — 4...Bc5. Black attacks d4 with the bishop, which occurs in 24% of the games. Let's check it out, starting with 1.e4!}
1. e4 e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its natural square in e4, e5 openings.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Black develops in the same manner. And now, after striking in the center with 3.d4, the Scotch is on the board.} 3. d4 exd4 {70% of the time, Black trades on d4. We'll recapture.} 4. Nxd4 Bc5 {This is the second most popular continuation, occurring in 24% of the games. Black adds an attacker to our knight on d4. We'll support our strong piece on d4 by bringing the bishop out to e3. [%csl Rd4]} 5. Be3 Qf6 {By moving the queen to f6, Black adds an attacker to the knight on d4. Alternatively, 5...Bb6 6.Nf5 is promising for us. We can add support to our central knight by playing c3. [%csl Rd4]} (5... Nxd4 {In 10% of the games, Black traded the knights on d4. We'll recapture with the bishop. [%csl Rd4]} 6. Bxd4 Bxd4 {Our bishop aimed at the weak g7-pawn, so Black trades also trades the bishops. We recapture. Our queen is nicely centralized now, and Black cannot attack it with any of their minor pieces. [%csl Rd4]} 7. Qxd4 Qf6 {...so they offer a queen trade. We'll decline, and attack the queen in return. Trading on f6 would also help Black develop the knight.} 8. e5 Qb6 {Black offers a queen trade yet again. Now we'll recapture. Black must damage their pawn structure and our pawn provides some spatial advantage for the endgame. [%csl Gd4,Rb6]} 9. Qxb6 axb6 {Black recaptures, opening the a-file for their rook. Our knight comes out. The d5-square looks great! We also clear the way for castling. [%cal Gb1c3] [%csl Rd5]} 10. Nc3 Ne7 {Black prevents us from jumping into d5. By castling queenside we get our king into safety and also bring the rook into the game. [%csl Rd5]} 11. O-O-O O-O {Black's king gets out of the center.} 12. Bc4 {c4 is a great square for the bishop: it aims at Black's king while also covering the a2-pawn. We'll continue by advancing on the kingside with g3-f4 and centralizing the h1-rook. [%cal Gg2g3,Gf2f4,Gh1e1] [%csl Ga2,Ra8]}) 6. c3 Nge7 {Black develops the knight to e7 and prepares to castle. We'll also prepare to castle by developing the bishop to c4. [%cal Ge8g8]} 7. Bc4 O-O {Nearly 40% of the time Black castles here. It's the most natural move in this position. We'll get the king into safety as well. Ideally, we want to trade off some of Black's most active pieces.} (7... d6 {In 20% of the games, Black continues with ...d6. They are opening up the c8-bishop for development, and protecting the other bishop on c5. We'll get the king into safety. [%csl Gc5,Gd6]} 8. O-O Bd7 {Black intends to castle queenside. We'll play Qd2, connecting the rooks and continuing to support the knight on d4. [%cal Ge8c8]} 9. Qd2 O-O-O {Black castles queenside.} 10. b4 {Our plan is pretty straightforward: a queenside pawn storm! We will play a4 and b5. It also comes with tempi on Black's minor pieces. [%cal Gb4b5,Ga2a4] [%csl Rc5,Rc8]}) (7... Ne5 {In 20% of the games, Black attacks the bishop on c4 by playing ...Ne5. We'll retreat our bishop to e2, taking away squares from the knight on e5. The idea is to play f4 without allowing ...Ng4. [%csl Rc4]} 8. Be2 Qg6 {Black attacks both the e4 and g2-pawns. This occured in 30% of the games. We'll castle and protect g2. [%csl Rg2,Re4]} (8... d5 {24% of games continue with Black striking in the center with ...d4, posing a question to our pawn on e4. We'll get the king into safety, sacrificing the pawn on e4 but in return, we'll get ahead in development. [%csl Re4]} 9. O-O dxe4 {Black grabs the "free" pawn, which was played in 70% of the games! Now Nb5 actually threatens a checkmate with Nxc7+ and Qd8#! [%cal Gd4b5] [%csl Rc7]} 10. Nb5 Bd6 {Black covers it. We'll set up a small trap with Bc5. Black cannot trade due to checkmate. [%csl Rc7]} 11. Bc5 O-O {Black must get their king out of the center now. We'll regain the material by trading on d6. [%csl Rd6]} 12. Nxd6 cxd6 {Black recaptures.} 13. Qxd6 {We've got the pawn back! We'll have control over the e-file, plus we are attacking e7 and threatening to damage Black's pawn structure with Qxf6. [%cal Gd6f6,Gg1d1,Rg7f6] [%csl Rf6,Re7]}) (8... d6 {Black could go for 8...d6, which occurs 20% of the time. We'll simply castle and get our king out of the center.} 9. O-O O-O {Black castles as well.} 10. f4 {And now we gain space on the kingside with f4, with the plans of expanding with b4 and finishing the development. [%cal Gb2b4,Gb1d2] [%csl Re5]}) 9. O-O Qxe4 {We lose a pawn but in return, our pieces will get rapid development. We'll start with Nd2, when the queen is attacked. [%csl Re4]} 10. Nd2 Qg6 {Black must move the queen yet again. Let's gain yet another tempo on the queen! [%csl Rg6]} 11. Bh5 Qd3 {Black tries to be annoying by keeping their queen in our camp. This happens in almost 60% of the games!} (11... Qb6 {Black creates a queen-bishop battery, which occurs almost 20% of the time. We'll immediately attack the bishop with b4. [%csl Gb6,Gc5]} 12. b4 Bxd4 {Black trades. Now we'll recapture with the bishop, hitting the knight on e5 and attacking the queen. [%csl Rb6,Re5]} 13. Bxd4 Qd6 {Black moves the queen.} 14. Re1 {The rook will be very potent on e1, as Black's king is still in the center and there is now an immediate attack on the e5-knight. [%cal Ge1e8] [%csl Re8,Re5]}) 12. N4f3 {We offer to trade the knights. After ....Nxf3+ Qxf3 and our position is great, with better development than Black. ...Bxe3 Nxe5 ...Qxd2 Bxf7+ and White is once again much better. [%cal Gd1f3,Re5f3] [%csl Re5]}) (7... Qg6 {This is the least popular move, played in less than 10% of the games. Black targets e4 and g2. We'll trade on c6. [%csl Rg2,Re4]} 8. Nxc6 Qxg2 {Black creates a threat against our rook... ...so we'll bring it to f1 and now Black's bishop on c5 is hanging! [%csl Rh1,Rc5]} 9. Rf1 Bxe3 {Black tries to avoid losing material.} 10. Nxe7 {...but we grab the knight on e7! White has won a piece here. [%cal Gf2e3,Re8e7] [%csl Ge7,Gc4]}) 8. O-O d6 {In a quarter of the games, Black opens up the light-squared bishop with ...d6. Moves like ...Bb6 or ...a6 don't really accomplish anything. The pressure against our knight on d4 hinders our development slightly, so we'll remove it by trading on c6. [%csl Gc8,Rc6]} (8... b6 {Very few games continue with ...b6. Now Qd2 prepares to connect the rooks and supports the bishop on e3.} 9. Qd2 Bb7 {Black fianchettoes. This places indirect pressure on the e4-pawn. [%csl Re4]} (9... Qg6 {Black decides to attack the pawn on e4 directly. We'll trade on c6, intending to trade off Black's powerful dark-squared bishop next. [%csl Re4,Rc6]} 10. Nxc6 Qxc6 {Black recaptures with the queen. We'll take on c5 as planned. [%csl Rc5]} 11. Bxc5 Qxc5 {If you compare the bishops, our bishop on c4 is much more active. [%csl Gc4,Gc8]} 12. Na3 {We finish our development by protecting the bishop on c4 and connecting the rooks. We will also centralize our rooks to gain greater control of the center. [%cal Ga1d1,Gf1e1] [%csl Gc4]}) 10. f4 {And here comes the classic f4-move, gaining some space on the kingside. Ideas of e5 and b4 are still present! [%cal Ge4e5,Gb2b4]}) 9. Nxc6 Nxc6 {Blacks keeps their structure intact and recaptures with the knight. Now we'll trade off the dark-squared bishop. It was Black's most active piece, and this trade also doubles Black's c-pawns. [%csl Rc5]} 10. Bxc5 dxc5 {Black recaptures. We'll gain some space in the center by pushing f4.} 11. f4 Be6 {Black offers to trade the light-squared bishops. We push forward, creating a threat against Black's queen. [%csl Rc4]} 12. e5 Qe7 {Black retreats the queen. We'll trade off the bishops, as retreating would be too passive. [%csl Re6]} 13. Bxe6 Qxe6 {Black recaptures with the queen. Our knight comes to d2, where it will either jump to e4 or f3. Both squares are suitable in this position.} 14. Nd2 Rad8 {Black pins our knight to the queen. So we'll unpin with Qc2. [%csl Rd2]} 15. Qc2 f6 {By playing ...f6, Black challenges our central pawns. [%csl Re5]} 16. exf6 {We'll trade since the pawn would be lost otherwise. The f-pawn provides some spatial advantage on the kingside and allows for a potential future attack. We have ideas of Nf3 or Ne4. The a-rook will side to e1 to gain control over the e-file. [%cal Gd2e4,Ga1e1,Gd2f3] [%csl Gf4]} *

[Chapter "The Schmidt Variation (4...Nf6)"]
[Study "Schmidt Variation (4...Nf6)"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Scotch Game/03-01 The Schmidt Variation (4...Nf6) - Schmidt Variation (4...Nf6).mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{Welcome to the third Study of the Scotch! In the previous chapter we covered 4...Bc5, but now we're going to take a look at the engine's top choice at the fourth move which is 4...Nf6, attacking our pawn on e4. Let's dive into it, starting with 1.e4!}
1. e4 e5 {Black copies us. We'll develop, attacking e5.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Black defends their e-pawn. With d4, our opening is now on the board! The Scotch.} 3. d4 exd4 {Black trades in the center. We'll recapture with the knight.} 4. Nxd4 Nf6 {And Black develops the knight to f6, eyeing our e4-pawn. At the club level, this is the third most popular move, but in the Masters database, it's the most played one. This is the engine's top choice. We'll trade off the knights. [%csl Re4]} 5. Nxc6 bxc6 {Black's pawns are doubled... ...and now we'll develop with Bd3, supporting the pawn on e4 and preparing to castle. [%csl Re4]} 6. Bd3 Bc5 {Black brings the bishop to c5, eyeing the f2-pawn. This move is not the strongest one, yet it is played 30% of the time. We will push e5 here, kicking the knight away from its natural square and opening up our light-squared bishop. [%cal Ge4e5] [%csl Rf6,Rf2]} (6... d5 {This is the most popular, and also the strongest move Black could go for, played in more than half of the games. Black immediately challenges our central pawn on e4. We'll trade here, getting rid of the pressure and opening our light-squared bishop. [%csl Re4]} 7. exd5 cxd5 {Black recaptures with the pawn. We'll get the king into safety and potentially the rook into action on the e-file.} 8. O-O Be7 {Black prepares to castle with ...Be7, occurring in almost 70% of the games. Our fianchetto on b2 will prove valuable against Black's kingside. [%cal Ge8g8]} (8... Bd6 {Black develops the bishop to d6, which is the second most popular move after ...Be7. We can improve our rook with a check. [%csl Re8]} 9. Re1+ Be6 {Black blocks the check with the light-squared bishop. Now that Black's light-squared bishop has abandoned its home square (and is pinned), we'll give a check on b5. [%csl Ge1,Re6,Re8]} (9... Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to e7, essentially losing a tempo. We'll create a queen-rook battery aimed at the dark-squared bishop, so that Black cannot castle immediately. [%csl Ge1,Re7]} 10. Qe2 Be6 {Black blocks the e-file. Now we'll give a check on b5, making use of the light squares yet again.} 11. Bb5+ Nd7 {Black blocks the check. We'll attack the rook and put pressure on the d5-pawn.} 12. Bc6 Rb8 {After Black moves the rook...} 13. Nc3 {...we develop the knight and soon the d5-pawn will fall. For example, 13...0-0 and we will capture with 14.Bxd5. We ideally want to take with the bishop so that our pressure on the e-file is felt a lot more. [%cal Gc6d5,Gc3d5] [%csl Rd5]}) 10. Bb5+ Nd7 {Black's only reasonable move is to cover the check with the knight. Now we'll attack both the d5-pawn and the rook on a8.} 11. Bc6 Rb8 {Black must move the rook. And now we can win a pawn and increase the pressure on the e6-bishop. [%csl Re6,Rd5]} 12. Bxd5 O-O {Black gets the king out of the center.} 13. Nc3 {And we develop the knight to c3 and support our strong bishop on d5. We are up a pawn and Black's queenside pawns are looking weak! [%cal Gd1f3] [%csl Gd5,Ra7,Rc7]}) 9. b3 O-O {Black castles. Now we'll play Bb2, with control along the long diagonal.} 10. Bb2 c5 {The pawn duo in the center for Black appears to be advantageous, but the position is equal. With h3, we ensure that Black won't get access to g4. [%csl Gc5,Gd5]} 11. h3 {We will now continue our development with moves like Re1 and Nd2. Bf1 can be played if we need it to put our bishop on a safe square and defend our kingside. This is an equal position, but our moves are easy to find. Black can make a strategic mistake with ...d4, giving us the c4-square as an outpost for a piece. [%cal Gb1d2,Gf1e1,Gd3f1,Rd5d4]}) 7. e5 Nd5 {Black jumps to d5, the only logical square for the knight. g4 is a great square for our queen now. It attacks the undefended g7-pawn and will pose some danger to Black if they castle kingside. [%csl Rg7]} (7... Qe7 {Black pins our pawn to the knight with the queen. We can see this in around 40% of the games. So, naturally, we'll castle, getting the king away from the pin. Black of course cannot grab the pawn on e5, because of the Re1 pin. [%csl Ge7,Re5,Re1]} 8. O-O Nd5 {Black moves the knight. We'll develop our knight. It will sit nicely on the e4-square.} 9. Nd2 O-O {Black gets the king into safety. We can try to win the bishop pair while centralizing our knight... [%cal Gd2e4] [%csl Rc5]} 10. Ne4 Bb6 {...so Black retreats. With c4, we'll gain more space on the queenside and get rid of Black's strong knight on d5. [%csl Rd5]} 11. c4 Nb4 {Black puts pressure on our bishop. Their pieces aren't developed well, meanwhile, all of our pieces aim at Black's kingside! This calls for a sacrifice. Now Nf6+ is a very powerful move. [%csl Rd3]} 12. Nf6+ gxf6 {Black grabs the material. And here comes another sacrifice! [%csl Rh7]} 13. Bxh7+ Kxh7 {Black captures. Now we bring the queen into the attack with check. Black's pawns prevent the king running away and limit the defensive scope of their queen!} 14. Qh5+ Kg8 {Black moves to g8. Check again! All of our moves should come with a check or a checkmate threat, so that Black does not have time to mobilize their defense. [%csl Rg8]} 15. Qg4+ Kh7 {The king moves. Another check, this time on h4! Now the dark-squared bishop will come in handy. [%csl Rh7]} 16. Qh4+ Kg8 {The king moves to g8. Now we threaten a checkmate in two. Black can't prevent the coming check on the g-file.} 17. Bh6 Qxe5 {This is Black's only resource not to get checkmated immediately. We still have the check on g4. [%csl Rg8]} 18. Qg4+ Qg5 {Black must cover with the queen, otherwise Qg7 is checkmate. [%csl Rg5]} 19. Bxg5 {But now we win the queen. [%cal Gf6g5,Gg4g5] [%csl Rg8]}) 8. Qg4 O-O {Black castles to defend the g7-pawn. Other moves are passive. Now Bh6 threatens a checkmate! Black of course cannot capture the bishop: it is pinned due to the good placement of our queen. [%cal Gc1h6] [%csl Gg4,Rg7,Rg8]} (8... g6 {This is the top move in the database, played 40% of the time. It defends g7, but creates some dark-squared weaknesses. We'll exploit them by placing our bishop on h6, preventing Black from castling. [%cal Gc1h6] [%csl Rf8,Rg7]} 9. Bh6 d6 {...d6 opens up an attack on our queen. We'll shift the queen to the queenside, attacking the c6-pawn. [%csl Rg4]} 10. Qa4 Bd7 {Black defends it. We'll get the king into safety and prepare to activate the rook. [%csl Rc6]} 11. O-O dxe5 {Black closes the e-file by capturing on e5.} 12. Bg7 {But now we get a strong hold of the center and quick development with Black's castling nonexistent! We will win a pawn and then it's up to us to consolidate by developing our pieces with moves like Nc3 and Re1, and holding on to the pawn advantage. [%cal Gg7e5,Gf1e1,Gb1c3] [%csl Rh8,Re5]}) 9. Bh6 g6 {This is the only move to defend and avoid major material losses. We can grab the rook now and be an exchange up. [%csl Rf8]} 10. Bxf8 Qxf8 {Black recaptures. Now it's time to get our king into safety.} 11. O-O d6 {Black opens up an attack on our queen. [%csl Rg4]} 12. Qe4 {The queen is nicely centralized on e4. We'll finish our development and enjoy the material advantage! Nd2 and Re1 are good, improving moves. If 12...Bf5, 13.Qa4 attacks c6 and we have a great position. [%cal Gb1d2,Gf1e1] [%csl Ge4]} *

[Chapter "Move 4 Sidelines"]
[Study "Move 4 Sidelines"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Scotch Game/04-01 Move 4 Sidelines - Move 4 Sidelines.mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{In Chapter 4 of our course on the Scotch, we will take a look at some sidelines on move 4 for Black, namely moving the queen to either f6 or h4, as well as striking in the center early with ...d5. Moving the queen is premature but can be dangerous, so we have to know how to respond. Let's begin with 1.e4.}
1. e4 e5 {Black responds with ...e5. The knight gets out and attacks the central pawn.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 {And Black defends it. With d4 we reach the Scotch Game.} 3. d4 exd4 {Black takes on d4. And we will recapture. All this we've seen before.} 4. Nxd4 Qf6 {The move ...Qf6 is probably positionally the best sideline. It's played 5% of the time, which is still almost 2.5 million games! Black is attacking our knight once again and often wants to slide to g6 to keep the queen active. We'll protect the knight for now, with c3. [%csl Rd4]} (4... Qh4 {The move ...Qh4 is played only 2% of the time, but does have some merit to it. In fact, Black has a bigger win rate than White in this line in the database. The queen attacks the e4-pawn. We can defend it with the knight, but Black has an answer to that too. [%csl Re4]} 5. Nc3 Bb4 {And that's to pin our defender. Now the threat on the knight is reintroduced. We don't want to complicate things by playing Nxc6, allowing Qe4+. In fact, the best move is to actually develop and pre-emptively block the incoming capture so that it no longer arrives with check. [%csl Rc3]} 6. Be2 Qxe4 {They can take the pawn here, but this is actually better for White. The queen leaving its post early has left the c7-pawn unattended. So we'll jump to b5 and attack the c7-pawn. It's actually not that easy to defend. [%csl Rc7]} 7. Nb5 Bxc3+ {Black takes on c3. We don't want to recapture with the knight and allow them to capture on g2. That would be bad for us. Instead, we'll take with the pawn and maintain the threat to c7. [%csl Rc7]} 8. bxc3 Kd8 {Now Black basically has to side-step to d8 and protect the pawn. Other than that, there aren't many good moves. [%csl Rc7]} 9. O-O {In that case, we will just castle, and let's take a look at what we got in this position. We are down a pawn, but we've acquired the bishop pair. Apart from that, we have more space to develop and a lead in activity, while Black has lost their castling rights. White is better here. [%csl Ge2,Gc1,Gb5]}) (4... d5 {The move ...d5 is only played 1% of the time, and it doesn't accomplish much for Black. We will take on d5 and release the unnecessary tension in the center. [%csl Rd5]} 5. exd5 Qxd5 {Sure, Black can activate their queen and attack the knight... [%csl Rd4]} 6. Qe2+ {But we don't have to play by their rules and permit a queen trade. The best move for Black is to block with the bishop on e6 and allow us to capture it. Otherwise, if they block with the bishop or the knight on e7, White is already winning after Nb5, when the queen would have to retreat passively to defend c7. [%cal Gc8e6,Gd4b5,Rf8e7,Rg8e7] [%csl Re8,Re6,Rc7]}) 5. c3 Qg6 {Capturing on d4 would be a mistake, giving us strong central pawns. Instead, a logical move is to move to g6, attacking e4, and freeing f6 for the knight. What they might not know is that we can let them capture the pawn! Because the queen has come out early, Nb5 is an extremely potent move, targeting the pawn on c7 and threatening a fork. [%cal Gd4b5] [%csl Re4,Rc7]} 6. Nb5 Qxe4+ {Even if Black captures on e4... It just gives us more room to develop, while blocking the check. You might notice that this move sacrifices the g2-pawn as well. Well, this double sacrifice is actually the way to go for White. [%csl Re1]} 7. Be2 Qxg2 {Because White is winning if Black takes this pawn. We can defend our rook with Bf3, also attacking the black queen, and the fork on c7 is still a problem for Black. [%csl Rc7,Rh1]} 8. Bf3 Qh3 {The queen has to escape to a square like h3... After which we can fork Black with the capture on c7. [%csl Rc7]} 9. Nxc7+ Kd8 {And once the king moves... We'll grab the rook. A good trade for two pawns. [%csl Ra8]} 10. Nxa8 Ne5 {Sure, Black could continue the aggression with ...Ne5, attacking our bishop. But they are playing with the only two pieces they have activated in the opening. [%csl Rf3]} 11. Be2 {We will just retreat and enjoy a winning position. Black has no more threats and we want to centralize the queen on d5 and play Bf4 to attack the knight and take the diagonal. It's not even going to be easy for Black to win our knight on a8. [%cal Gc1f4,Gd1d5]} *

