{Hello and welcome to the fifth chapter! In the last chapter, Black deviated on the fourth move, since they've still continued to trade on d4. In this study, we'll check out some of Black's moves that avoid capturing on d4. We'll begin with 1.e4.}
1. e4 e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We'll develop the knight, attacking e5.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Black develops the knight and supports e5. Now 3.d4 is the Scotch!} 3. d4 d6 {This move is played only 10% of the time, but it is the second most popular move! Black's idea is to support the e5-pawn and avoid opening up the center. We can now gain space in the center with a tempo on Black's c6-knight. [%csl Gd6,Ge5]} (3... Nf6 {Black develops the knight, counter-attacking our pawn on e4. This is the third most popular move, occurring 7% of the time, representing over 1 million games! We'll win a pawn on e5 and attack the knight. [%csl Re4]} 4. dxe5 Nxe4 {Black regains the pawn. Now c4 is a great square for our light-squared bishop. We immediately start to eye the weak f7-pawn. There are ideas of bringing the queen to d5 and threatening checkmate! [%csl Rf7]} 5. Bc4 Bc5 {Black piles up on the f2-square. But f7 is also a target. With Qd5, we continue with our plan, and a checkmate is now threatened! [%csl Rf2,Rf7]} (5... d6 {Black offers another pawn for a quick development. ...d6 is the fourth most popular move. Now we can sacrifice our bishop on f7. The motif of bringing our queen to d5 is apparent yet again! [%csl Rf7]} 6. Bxf7+ Kxf7 {Black recaptures. Our queen now arrives on d5 with check, forking the king and the e4-knight. [%cal Gd1d5] [%csl Re4,Rf7]} 7. Qd5+ Be6 {Black attacks our queen and blocks the check. We'll regain the piece. [%csl Rd5]} 8. Qxe4 dxe5 {Black recaptures on e5 with the pawn to free up their pieces and go for some activity. 8...Be7 was also possible. But their king isn't safe! We must keep attacking here, or Black will escape. Thus we'll play Ng5+.} 9. Ng5+ Ke7 {Blacks moves the king and must also guard the e6-bishop. We'll grab the bishop, luring Black's king into the open. [%csl Re6]} 10. Nxe6 Kxe6 {Black recaptures. [%csl Re6]} 11. Nc3 {We can just develop here. Black's king is feeling very exposed and our knight will most likely end up on the d5-square. We can choose to castle either side, depending on Black's plan of hiding their king! [%cal Gc3d5,Gc1e3,Ge1c1,Ge1g1] [%csl Re6]}) 6. Qd5 Bxf2+ {Black captures on f2 with a check...} 7. Ke2 {...but after Ke2, Black's positions falls apart, since they cannot defend both of their pieces and a checkmate threat on f7 at the same time. [%cal Gd5f7,Gc4f7] [%csl Ge2,Gd5,Re4,Rf2,Rf7]}) (3... d5 {Black goes for a central counter-attack with 3...d5. This move occurs very rarely. We can grab the pawn on e5, getting our knight to a strong square at the same time. [%csl Re4]} 4. Nxe5 Nxe5 {Black trades, which happens in 60% of the time. We'll recapture the knight. [%csl Re5]} (4... dxe4 {Black continues with the second most popular move which is grabbing the pawn on e4. We'll put pressure on the c6-knight by pinning it. [%csl Rc6]} 5. Bb5 Bd7 {Black defends it with the bishop. As Black was threatening to capture the knight on e5, we'll get rid of the bishop and keep the knight on c6 pinned! [%csl Rd7]} 6. Nxd7 Qxd7 {Black recaptures.} 7. c3 {And with c3 we support the pawn on d4 while preparing to target the e4-pawn by developing and castling kingside. The c6-knight remains pinned. [%cal Ge1g1,Gb1d2] [%csl Gb5,Re4,Rc6]}) 5. dxe5 dxe4 {Black must regain the pawn, or they'll just be a pawn down without any real compensation. Trading the queens is good for us here, since Black will lose their castling rights in an otherwise symmetrical position. [%csl Rd8]} 6. Qxd8+ Kxd8 {Black recaptures the queen.} 7. Nc3 {And after developing the knight we'll target the e4-pawn, bring the bishops to c4 and g5 and enjoy our castling rights! [%cal Gf1c4,Gc1g5,Ge1c1] [%csl Re4,Rd8]}) 4. d5 Nce7 {Back retreats to e7 instead of jumping straight back to b8. If instead ...Nb4, we have a3, and after ...Na6 then Bxa6 when we have seriously damaged Black's pawn structure. After Nce7 we'll add to our pawn center with c4.} 5. c4 Nf6 {Black brings out the knight with an attack on the e4-pawn. We'll develop. c3 is a natural square for the knight. [%csl Re4]} 6. Nc3 Bg4 {Black develops the bishop to g4, pinning our knight on f3. We'll ask the bishop a question with h3. [%csl Rf3]} 7. h3 Bh5 {Black retreats to h5 to keep the pin. We'll expand on the kingside and gain time on the bishop. [%csl Rf3]} (7... Bxf3 {Instead of retreating, Black immediately captures the knight on f3 which happens in around 50% of the games. We should recapture with the queen to keep our pawn structure intact. [%csl Rf3]} 8. Qxf3 Ng6 {Black improves the knight and opens up e7 for the bishop. Against a knight on g6, the h4 push is usually very powerful! We are threatening to push even further with h5, attacking the knight on g6. [%cal Gf8e7]} 9. h4 h5 {Black stops our intended advance. Now g3 acomplishes three things: supports the pawn on h4, protects the f4-square and allows for our light-squared bishop to be developed.} 10. g3 Be7 {Black prepares to castle. Black is without a light-squared bishop, so our own bishop is a very powerful piece! We'll activate it on h3.} 11. Bh3 O-O {Black gets their king into safety.} 12. Bg5 {After Bg5 we can plan to capture the knight on f6 and grab the pawn on h5 next. White is doing very well here, as we have a space advantage on the kingside, and our pieces are better placed than Black's. [%cal Gg5f6,Gf3h5] [%csl Rf8,Rh5]}) 8. g4 Bg6 {They retreat to g6. Now we develop a piece with Bd3 and support the pawn on e4. [%csl Re4]} 9. Bd3 c6 {Black tries to undermine our pawn chain. [%csl Rd5]} 10. Be3 {But we can simply bring yet another piece to a better square. Black's pieces are looking very cramped on the kingside since we have a space advantage there. Our plan can revolve around gaining further space there and hiding our king on the queenside. [%cal Gf3h4,Gh4f5,Gd1e2,Ge1c1] [%csl Re8,Rf8,Re7,Rg6,Rf6]} *
